# CR 702.192a, 603.7, 707.10a and the paradigm rulings ("Once a spell with paradigm has resolved, it doesn't matter what
# happens to the card in exile. Even if the card leaves exile, the delayed triggered ability will still trigger during each of
# your first main phases, and the copy will still be created"; a copy that isn't cast "will cease to exist the next time
# state-based actions are checked"). Paradigm's delayed trigger is made by the first spell of the name its controller resolves -
# a copy too - and lives on no card: here no Germination Practicum is ever in exile.
#
# Turn 3: Bot1 casts Germination Practicum and, holding it on the stack, flashes in Lutri, the Spellchaser, whose enters trigger
# copies it (CR 707.10). The copy resolves first: two +1/+1 counters on Llanowar Elves (3/3) and Lutri (5/4), and, being the
# first Practicum to resolve, its paradigm makes the delayed trigger; the copy then ceases to exist. Bot2 counters the card
# itself with Counterspell: it goes to the graveyard, not exile, and makes no trigger. Turn 5: the trigger still offers a copy,
# created from the spell as it resolved; cast, it makes the Elves 5/5 and Lutri 7/6. Turn 7: the copy is declined, so it
# ceases to exist (exile stays empty) and nothing grows. Were the trigger tied to a card in exile there would be no offer on
# turn 5 (the Elves 3/3); were the declined copy left in exile, Exile.Count would be 1.

